# Untitled

What the duct: Imaging ductular reaction spanning the fibrotic areas in primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC)
a r t i c l e i n f o
## 1. Introduction
Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is a heterogenous and idiopathic type of chronic liver disease that targets the epithelial cells (i.e., cholangiocytes) lining the bile ducts in the liver [1,2]. The pathogenesis of PSC is undetermined, yet genetic and environmental factors are widely regarded as contributors to both the disease progression and outcome [1,3]. Additionally, 70–80% of PSC patients are also diagnosed with inflammatory bowel disease, indicating a role for gut-derived factors and microbiome dysregulation in PSC etiopathogenesis [1]. There are limited treatment options available for PSC patients, and approximately 40% of PSC patients require liver transplant, as it is one of the few viable options [1]. Cholangiocytes vary in size and function based on location in either small ducts or large ducts, and PSC is known to affect both small and large bile ducts and results in progressive biliary inflammation [1]. Following injury, small and large cholangiocytes proliferate and begin to synthesize and secrete a plethora of factors that influence the surrounding environment through paracrine interactions [4]. While biliary involvement in fibroinflammatory response during PSC is known, it is unclear of the full involvement of cholangiocytes in this process. Considering the unknown etiopathogenesis and lack of curative options, it is imperative that we understand the underlying biological processes driving PSC progression to find new targetable pathways.One key hallmark of PSC is the presence of ductular reaction (DR), a phenomenon defined as the proliferation of reactive bile ducts, following liver injury, that are demarcated as misshapen, tortuous and lacking a lumen [3,5]. DR leads to the expansion of the biliary compartment composed of both cholangiocytes and liver progenitor cells, which is postulated to aid in wound-healing responses and parenchymal regeneration [6]. Conversely, the extent of DR is often directly related to degree of liver damage and patient's health outcomes [5]. Moreover, DR is associated with liver fibrosis, another characteristic of PSC, due to the ability of DR cells to directly activate hepatic stellate cells (main fibrogenic cell of the liver) and induce fibrogenesis [5].
One key hallmark of PSC is the presence of ductular reaction (DR), a phenomenon defined as the proliferation of reactive bile ducts, following liver injury, that are demarcated as misshapen, tortuous and lacking a lumen [3,5]. DR leads to the expansion of the biliary compartment composed of both cholangiocytes and liver progenitor cells, which is postulated to aid in wound-healing responses and parenchymal regeneration [6]. Conversely, the extent of DR is often directly related to degree of liver damage and patient's health outcomes [5]. Moreover, DR is associated with liver fibrosis, another characteristic of PSC, due to the ability of DR cells to directly activate hepatic stellate cells (main fibrogenic cell of the liver) and induce fibrogenesis [5].
Therefore, it is unclear if DR is a protective or damaging mechanism during PSC progression. Periductular accumulation of collagen in PSC patients leads to narrowing segments of bile ducts called biliary strictures, which cause bile retention and further inflammation through increased toxic bile acid-induced damage [1,3]. Peribiliary fibrosis can evolve into bridging fibrosis, liver cirrhosis and end-stage liver disease, as well as put patients at higher risk for developing cholangiocarcinoma, which is a rare liver cancer arising from the biliary compartment [7].
Given that DR and biliary fibrosis play key roles in PSC progression, it is of great interest to study the interplay between proliferating cholangiocytes and fibrogenesis; thus, we developed a co-staining protocol to visually observe this interaction. The current cover of BBA Molecular Basis of Disease shows the expansion of DR within the fibrotic septa in a PSC patient sample. Immunohistochemical staining marked CK-19-positive cholangiocytes that were stained brown to highlight the bile ducts. Following chromogen development, Fast Green Sirius Red (FGSR) was utilized to counterstain the tissue green, in lieu of Hematoxylin, and also stain collagen deposits red. Therefore, the image showcases extensive DR located specifically within regions of bridging fibrosis, which may be an important indicator of disease progression.
