is. For instance, staining of various immune cells could be used to image immune cell infiltration with liver fibrosis. Outside of the liver, this co-staining technique can be applied to other organs and tissues. Future applications of this protocol will allow for further studies on the association and location of nearly any cell types in respect to fibrosis.
## 2. Methods
2. MethodsDeidentified human liver tissue from patients diagnosed with late-stage PSC were collected by Dr. Burcin Esker at Indiana University School of Medicine following written informed consent and IRB approval. Liver samples were processed, embedded into paraffin blocks, and sectioned at \(5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) for staining. Immunohistochemistry staining of bile ducts using anti-CK-19 antibody (Abcam; Waltham, MA), Vector ImmPRESS® HRP Reagent Kit and chromogen development using the Vector ImmPACT® DAB substrate (Vector Laboratories; Burlingame, CA) was performed. FGSR staining solution is made by adding Picrosirius Red \((0.1\%)\) and Fast Green FCF \((0.1\%)\) to saturated picric acid \((1.2\%~w/v)\), and slides are incubated in the pre-made solution for 30 min. We chose to use FGSR staining since it provides well-defined collagen fibers and the highest sensitivity for quantitative analysis compared to Sirius Red alone [8]. Lastly, slides are cleared, mounted, scanned using the Lecia Aperio AT2 system and imaged with the Lecia Aperio ImageScope software (Leica Biosystems; Buffalo Grove, IL).
